{"task": {"id": "I_kwDOUDROfM8AAAABVFjEVQ", "item_id": "PVTI_lAHOAHSLSM4BlR_zzg-mXRA", "project_id": "PVT_kwHOAHSLSM4BlR_z", "number": 35, "title": "Make ReazonSpeech simulated streaming follow hayamimi-style utterance handling", "body": "## Problem

Issue #33 added a simple ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming mode using cumulative previews and fixed 10-second windows.

Manual use suggests that this mode can feel less accurate than the existing offline ReazonSpeech path. A likely cause is that fixed windows can split speech at arbitrary boundaries and discard useful context.

The [hayamimi](https://github.com/oboroge0/hayamimi) project uses the same general Japanese stack (ReazonSpeech + sherpa-onnx) with a more speech-aware realtime pipeline.

We want to adapt the small set of utterance-handling techniques that are most relevant to the current browser experiment.

## Expected outcome

Replace the current fixed-window simulated-streaming policy with a hayamimi-inspired utterance policy:

```text
microphone
  ↓
VAD
  ↓
speech start + 0.8 s pre-roll
  ↓
while speaking: provisional ReazonSpeech decode about every 0.5 s
  ↓
0.35 s silence
  ↓
final ReazonSpeech decode of the utterance
  ↓
force-finalize if speech reaches 12 s
```

Implement these five behaviors together:

1. speech-aware VAD segmentation;
2. approximately 0.8 seconds of pre-roll;
3. provisional ReazonSpeech decoding approximately every 0.5 seconds while speech is active;
4. final decoding after approximately 0.35 seconds of trailing silence;
5. a 12-second maximum utterance duration that forces finalization.

Keep the existing ReazonSpeech model/runtime unchanged.

Completion criteria must be executable and verifiable by an AI agent. Subjective human comparison of recognition quality remains optional additional validation.

## Scope

### 1. VAD-based utterance segmentation

Replace fixed 10-second final windows in the simulated-streaming mode with speech-aware segmentation.

Use a browser-compatible VAD suitable for this experiment. Prefer Silero VAD if it can be integrated without disproportionate complexity, because hayamimi uses Silero VAD.

If a different implementation is required for the Web/WASM environment, document the reason while preserving the intended behavior.

Do not change the existing offline ReazonSpeech mode.

### 2. 0.8-second pre-roll

Maintain a rolling audio buffer before detected speech.

When speech starts, prepend approximately 0.8 seconds of preceding audio to the utterance so VAD onset latency does not clip the beginning of speech.

The buffer must remain bounded.

### 3. Approximately 0.5-second provisional updates

While an utterance is active, periodically run the existing offline ReazonSpeech recognizer against the current utterance audio.

Target approximately 0.5 seconds between eligible provisional updates.

Only one ReazonSpeech decode should run at a time. If inference is slower than incoming audio, coalesce or skip superseded provisional work rather than building an unbounded queue.

Provisional text must remain clearly marked as unstable.

### 4. Finalize after approximately 0.35 seconds of silence

When VAD observes approximately 0.35 seconds of trailing silence after active speech, finalize the utterance.

Run a final ReazonSpeech decode using the complete utterance audio, including pre-roll and appropriate trailing audio.

Commit that result as final text and begin waiting for the next utterance.

### 5. Maximum utterance duration of 12 seconds

If continuous speech reaches approximately 12 seconds without a VAD endpoint, force-finalize the current utterance.

Start a new utterance cleanly without losing or duplicating audio around the boundary.

## Pre-merge acceptance criteria

Use deterministic audio fixtures and/or controlled VAD/worker fixtures so the behavior is agent-verifiable.

Verify that:

- silence alone does not create a committed utterance;
- detected speech includes approximately 0.8 seconds of available pre-roll;
- an active utterance becomes eligible for provisional decoding approximately every 0.5 seconds;
- provisional inference never creates an unbounded decode queue;
- approximately 0.35 seconds of trailing silence triggers finalization;
- the final decode receives the complete intended utterance audio rather than a fixed arbitrary 10-second slice;
- continuous speech at approximately 12 seconds is force-finalized;
- audio around the forced boundary is neither lost nor duplicated;
- Stop flushes any active utterance into a final result;
- Cancel, repeat recording, and backend switching do not leak stale results;
- the existing offline ReazonSpeech mode remains unchanged;
- Moonshine, two-pass, ReazonSpeech offline, and Whisper modes continue to work;
- existing relevant unit/browser tests pass.

Where practical, an opt-in real-model test may exercise ReazonSpeech with deterministic Japanese WAV input. Subjective accuracy improvement is not required for Issue completion.

## Optional manual validation

After implementation, compare the previous #33 behavior conceptually with the new mode using representative Japanese speech.

Useful observations include:

- first provisional latency;
- provisional update frequency;
- whether word beginnings are clipped;
- finalization latency after a pause;
- recognition quality compared with offline ReazonSpeech;
- behavior during speech longer than 12 seconds;
- CPU responsiveness and thermal behavior.

These observations are optional and do not block completion.

## Required post-merge verification

None.

## Out of scope

Do not add other hayamimi features in this Issue, including:

- second-pass delayed re-decoding;
- punctuation restoration;
- numeric normalization;
- leading-loss detection and retry heuristics;
- translation;
- diarization;
- multilingual routing;
- changing the ReazonSpeech model;
- Personalized ASR;
- a general-purpose ASR pipeline framework.

Those can be evaluated separately if the five core utterance-handling changes are promising.

## Context

Related:

- #33 — initial ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming experiment
- #2 — earlier design notes inspired by hayamimi
- hayamimi reference: https://github.com/oboroge0/hayamimi

The purpose is not to copy hayamimi wholesale. It is to test whether its core speech-aware segmentation strategy improves the current browser ReazonSpeech experiment while keeping the implementation focused and replaceable.
